From 0628b95ceee9dd247c92e0eeb84cd8cf8d2461d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian 'redbeard' Harrington <redbeard@dead-city.org>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 17:07:41 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] Silicon/Spacemit/K3/DwEqosDxe: leave EMAC clocks enabled
 at ExitBootServices

The ExitBootServices path (EqosNotifyExitBootServices -> EqosStop ->
SpacemitAssertReset) gated the EMAC bus clocks on the way to the OS.
Under ACPI the OS has no clock driver, so the GMAC arrived dead and
the first driver probe found the control register zeroed. Make
SpacemitAssertReset a no-op for the EMAC clocks so the handoff state
stays working; the MAC itself is still quiesced.

Validated on hardware: GMAC enumerates via PRP0001 (dwmac-spacemit)
and reaches DHCP without any kernel-side clock re-enable.
---
 .../Spacemit/K3/Drivers/DwEqosDxe/DwEqosSpacemit.c   | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/Silicon/Spacemit/K3/Drivers/DwEqosDxe/DwEqosSpacemit.c b/Silicon/Spacemit/K3/Drivers/DwEqosDxe/DwEqosSpacemit.c
index 92c05530..cc50e804 100644
--- a/Silicon/Spacemit/K3/Drivers/DwEqosDxe/DwEqosSpacemit.c
+++ b/Silicon/Spacemit/K3/Drivers/DwEqosDxe/DwEqosSpacemit.c
@@ -877,7 +877,17 @@ SpacemitAssertReset (
     return EFI_INVALID_PARAMETER;
   }
 
-  EqosClockDisable (Priv, Priv->BusClkAndRstName);
+  //
+  // Do NOT disable the EMAC bus clock here. This "reset" is invoked from
+  // EqosStop() when the EFI network stack is torn down at ExitBootServices,
+  // and gating the bus clock makes the MAC MMIO region stop responding: an
+  // ACPI OS without a clock provider (the clock is owned by firmware) wedges
+  // the bus on the first register access. Firmware owns the clocks and must
+  // leave the device usable for the OS it boots.
+  //
+  // The Linux dwmac-spacemit driver re-programming the MAC (reset, DMA
+  // reinit) does not require the clock to be toggled.
+  //
 
   return EFI_SUCCESS;
 }
